The 5 SOLID Principles
-----------------------

S - Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
A class should have one, and only one, reason to change.
In Java: Instead of one Employee class that handles database logic, email notifications, and salary calculations,
split them into specialized classes like EmployeeRepository, EmailService, and SalaryCalculator.

O - Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.
In Java: Use interfaces or abstract classes to add new functionality without changing existing code. For example,
a Discount interface allows you to add BlackFridayDiscount or VipDiscount classes without editing the main Checkout logic.

L - Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)
Subclasses should be substitutable for their base classes without breaking the application.
In Java: A classic violation is a Square class extending a Rectangle class. Since a square forces its width and height to be equal,
it might break logic that expects to set them independently on a rectangle.

I - Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use.
In Java: Instead of one massive Worker interface with work(), eat(), and sleep(), create smaller, specific interfaces like Workable and Feedable. 
This prevents a Robot class from being forced to implement an eat() method.

D - Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
High-level modules should depend on abstractions, not on low-level concrete implementations.
In Java: A Switch class should depend on a Switchable interface rather than a specific LightBulb class.
This allows the switch to operate anything—a fan, a heater, or a bulb—without modifying the Switch code
